			<item> <title>The Invite, 2026</title> <link>https://letterboxd.com/mbill/film/the-invite-2026/</link> <guid isPermaLink="false">letterboxd-review-1555244513</guid> <pubDate>Mon, 26 Oct 2026 17:16:08 +1300</pubDate> <letterboxd:watchedDate>2026-10-26</letterboxd:watchedDate> <letterboxd:rewatch>No</letterboxd:rewatch> <letterboxd:filmTitle>The Invite</letterboxd:filmTitle> <letterboxd:filmYear>2026</letterboxd:filmYear> <letterboxd:memberLike>No</letterboxd:memberLike> <tmdb:movieId>950028</tmdb:movieId> <description><![CDATA[ <p><img src="https://a.ltrbxd.com/resized/film-poster/8/5/4/8/3/1/854831-the-invite-2026-0-600-0-900-crop.jpg?v=ee72905e48"/></p> <p>I recently got to listen to Seth Rogen speak at TIFF, and it really grew my respect for him. He seems to truly enjoy his work, and it shows. After hearing him, it really is no surprise as to why he&#039;s as successful as he is. Not super relevant because he&#039;s primarily acting here, but I wanted to share it somewhere.</p><p>Anyways, he&#039;s only a fourth of this cast, and they all do a fantastic job. The relationship between Rogen and Wilde are the highlight of the movie, their arguments, their dynamic, their semi-paradoxical chemistry, it shines. The weakest part of the film for me is exactly when they split apart.</p><p>I would be curious to see how this film works on a rewatch, just because it weaves such a specific pacing and narrative flow, but I&#039;m not sure it would have the same weight in its reveals. Still, glad I finally doubles back on this one. Gotta check out Bugonia next before I get spoiled eventually.</p> ]]></description> <dc:creator>MBill</dc:creator> </item>
		
